==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|pétrifier|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{term|petrificare||lang=la}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|petra||lang=la|rock}} + {{term|-ficare||lang=la}} from {{term|facere||lang=la|do, make}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈpɛ.tɹəˌfaɪ/|lang=en}}

===Verb===
[[Image:Petrified tree Kirstenbosch.jpg|thumb|petrified tree Kirstenbosch Cape Town]]
{{en-verb|petrifies|petrifying|petrified}}

# To harden [[organic]] matter by [[permeate|permeating]] with water and depositing dissolved [[mineral]]s.
#* {{rfdate|and other bibliographic particulars}} Kirwan
#*: a river that '''petrifies''' any sort of wood or leaves
# To produce rigidity akin to stone.
# To [[immobilize]] with [[fright]].
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To become stone, or of a stony hardness, as organic matter by calcareous deposits.
# {{context|intransitive|figurative|lang=en}} To become stony, [[callous]], or [[obdurate]].
#* {{rfdate|and other bibliographic particulars}} Dryden
#*: Like Niobe we marble grow, / And '''petrify''' with grief.
# {{context|transitive|figurative|lang=en}} To make callous or obdurate; to stupefy; to paralyze; to transform; as by petrification.
#* {{rfdate|and other bibliographic particulars}} {{w|Alexander Pope}}
#*: '''petrify''' a genius to a dunce
#* {{rfdate|and other bibliographic particulars}} {{w|George Eliot}}
#*: A hideous fatalism, which ought, logically, to '''petrify''' your volition.

====Synonyms====
